Cloakroom
=========

The scripts, Compose file, Dockerfile, and examples in this repository are
licensed under the MIT License (see LICENSE).

The browser in Cloakroom's image (ghcr.io/jonclegg/cloakroom) is the Fury core:
Chromium with Fury's fingerprint patches, built for Linux from the fork at
https://github.com/jonclegg/fury-antidetect-browser/tree/linux-build and
downloaded at image build time from that fork's releases.

- Chromium is BSD-3-Clause licensed by The Chromium Authors, with third-party
  components under their own licenses (listed at chrome://credits in the
  browser).
- Fury's patch series (core/patches in the fork) is BSD-3-Clause licensed by
  The Fury Authors. Its build scripts and the rest of the Fury repository are
  AGPL-3.0-or-later; none of them are in the image.
- The core is built with proprietary_codecs (H.264, AAC), as Chrome is.
- fury-personas.json is derived from Fury's persona catalogue
  (shared-rs/src/catalogue.rs, AGPL-3.0-or-later) by its `catalogue` example.

The image also contains the MIT-licensed CloakBrowser wrapper code (for
cloakserve, the browser pool, GeoIP, and native input), from the fork at
https://github.com/jonclegg/CloakBrowser/tree/cloakroom-fixes. It does not
contain or download CloakHQ's proprietary CloakBrowser binary.

Cloakroom is an independent community project and is not affiliated with or
endorsed by CloakHQ or the Fury authors.

The optional in-browser viewer uses noVNC (MPL-2.0), websockify (LGPL-3.0),
and x11vnc (GPL-2.0), installed from Debian packages at build time.
websockify and x11vnc are unmodified. noVNC is unmodified except that the
build adds one hidden element to its vnc.html (see image/Dockerfile) so
browsers holding a cached older noVNC script still load. noVNC's vnc.html is
BSD-2-Clause per its file header; the rest of noVNC is MPL-2.0.
